          .maruku_toc
          %h1#idea Idea
          %p
            Although the definition of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%B6licher+space"} Frölicher space
            does not use a topology, it is topological in flavour and there are many topological concepts that can be defined for Frölicher spaces. In other pages, we have used the notion of a Hausdorff Frölicher space and have used a vague notion of a topology on the functions on a Frölicher space. In this page, we shall investigate the connections between the theory of Frölicher spaces and topological spaces a little more consistently.
          %p
            There are two different ways of thinking of topological notions on Frölicher spaces. One says that there is a functor (actually two functors) from the category of Frölicher space to the category of topological spaces so we can say that a Frölicher space has topological property
            %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            if the corresponding topological space has it. The other approach says that we can directly define a property for Frölicher spaces that is
            %em analogous
            to a topological property. We then might hope for a theorem saying that a Frölicher space with property
            %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            defines a topological space with the corresponding property
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p The first approach involves defining a functor (or more than one) from the category of Frölicher spaces to that of topological spaces and looking at the image. The second approach involves comparing the two categories and trying to transfer ideas from the more well-known category of topological spaces to that of Frölicher spaces. We shall study both.
          %h1#functors Functors
          %p Let us start by defining the two functors to topological spaces.
          #FroTop.num_defn{:style => "border: solid #cccccc; border-width: 2px 1px; padding: 0 1em; margin: 0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The
              %em curvaceous
              topology on a Frölicher space is the strongest topology for which the smooth curves are continuous.
            %p
              The
              %em functional
              topology on a Frölicher space is the weakest topology for which the smooth functions are continuous.
          %p It is clear that these assignments are functorial, and that the curvaceous topology is always at least as strong as the functional topology.
          %p As it is an inductive topology, the curvaceous topology has the following characterisation.
          #LemCurvTop.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,C_X,F_X)
              be a Frölicher space. A subset
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq X
              is open in the curvaceous topology if and only if
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^{-1}(U)
              is open in
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C_X
            %p
              Thus a subset
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
              is not open if and only if there is some
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C_X
              such that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^{-1}(A)
              is not open in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              We merely need to observe that the family
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext open
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U : c^{-1}(U) \;\text{open}\; \forall c \in C_X\}
              defines a topology. This is elementary.
          %p
            The functional topology is a projective topology and so it is
            %em generated
            by sets of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(a,b)
            %span.newWikiWord
              subbasis
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/subbasis"} ?
            for the topology. Using the structure of the functionals, we can strengthen that.
          #LemFunTop.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,C_X,F_X)
              be a Frölicher space. A subset
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq X
              is open in the functional topology if and only if for each
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in U
              there is a function
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in F_X
              such that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_19.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(p) = 1
              and
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_20.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(q) = 0
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \notin U
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              The “if” is obvious so we prove the “only if”. Thus let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq X
              be open and
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in U
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1, \dots, f_n
              and
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_25.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i, b_i \in \mathbb{R}
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_26.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mi i
                    %msubsup
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi U
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    p \in \bigcap_i f_i^{-1}(a_i,b_i) \subseteq U\;.
            %p
              We combine the functions
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i
              into a single function
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \coloneqq (f_1, \dots, f_n) \colon X \to \mathbb{R}^n
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mi i
                    %msubsup
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi …
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\bigcap_i f_i^{-1}(a_i,b_i) = f^{-1} (a_1,b_1) \times \dots \times (a_n,b_n)
            %p
              Now we use the fact that the topology on
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              is smoothly regular to find a smooth function
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \colon \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}
              such that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(f(p)) = 1
              and
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(y) = 0
              outside the specified rectangle. Then the composition
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ f
              has the required properties.
          %h1#basic_properties Basic Properties
          %p Let us start with some very simple definitions.
          #DiscIndisc.num_defn{:style => "border: solid #cccccc; border-width: 2px 1px; padding: 0 1em; margin: 0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A Frölicher space is said to be
              %em indiscrete
              if all curves are smooth.
            %p
              A Frölicher space is said to be
              %em discrete
              if all functions are smooth.
          %p Let us observe that there is no need for functional or curvaceous versions of these definitions.
          #LemDiscIndisc.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_3 Lemma
            %p A Frölicher space is indiscrete if and only if the only smooth functions are the constant ones.
            %p A Frölicher space is discrete if and only if the only smooth curves are the constant ones.
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              If all curves are smooth then for any points
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in X
              the curve
            #eq:eq1.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mrow
                        %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi x
                            %mtd
                              %mi t
                              %mo ≤
                              %mn 0
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi y
                            %mtd
                              %mi t
                              %mo ≥
                              %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\alpha(t) = \begin{cases}
                    x &amp; t \leq 0 \\
                    y &amp; t \ge 0
                    \\end{cases}
            %p
              is smooth. Composition with
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              yields the function
            #eq:eq2.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ↦
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mrow
                        %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mi t
                              %mo ≤
                              %mn 0
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mi t
                              %mo ≥
                              %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    t \mapsto \begin{cases}
                    \\phi(x) &amp; t \leq 0 \\
                    \\phi(y) &amp; t \ge 0
                    \\end{cases}
            %p
              For this to be a smooth function in
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\mathbb{R}, \mathbb{R})
              we must have
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_40.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x) = \phi(y)
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_41.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              is constant.
            %p
              For the converse, if the only smooth functions are constant then any curve
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_42.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : \mathbb{R} \to X
              satisfies the condition that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_43.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \circ \alpha \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}, \mathbb{R})
              for all
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_44.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              so is in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_45.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %p The discrete case is similar.
          %h1#separation_axioms Separation Axioms
          %p
            In other sections, namely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/examples+of+Fr%C3%B6licher+spaces"} examples of Frölicher spaces
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%B6licher+spaces+and+Isbell+envelopes"} Frölicher spaces and Isbell envelopes
            we used the notion of a
            %em Hausdorff
            Frölicher space. Technically, we ought to have called that
            %em functionally Hausdorff
            as it used the smooth functions in its definition.
          #DefFroHausdorff.num_defn{:style => "border: solid #cccccc; border-width: 2px 1px; padding: 0 1em; margin: 0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A Frölicher space is said to be
              %em functionally Hausdorff
              if the smooth functions separate points.
            %p
              A Frölicher space is said to be
              %em curvaceously Hausdorff
              if the only smooth curves with finite image are constant.
          %p However, the distinction is not important as the following lemma shows.
          #LemFroHausdorff.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_4 Lemma
            %p The notions of functional Hausdorff and curvaceously Hausdorff coincide and are equivalent to the underlying topological spaces being Hausdorff.
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              Suppose that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_46.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,C,F)
              is not functionally Hausdorff. Then there are
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_47.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne y \in X
              such that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_48.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x) = \phi(y)
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_49.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_50.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : \mathbb{R} \to X
              be the function taking the value
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_51.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              for
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_52.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \leq 0
              and
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_53.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_54.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \ge 0
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_55.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \circ \alpha
              is constant for all
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_56.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              so
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_57.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in C
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_58.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              has finite image but is not constant. Thus
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_59.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,C,F)
              is not curvaceously Hausdorff.
            %p
              Conversely, suppose that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_60.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,C,F)
              is not curvaceously Hausdorff. Then there is some
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_61.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in C
              with finite image which is not constant. Let
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_62.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_63.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \circ \alpha
              has finite image in
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_64.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              and hence is constant. Thus for
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_65.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} im
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in \im \alpha
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_66.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x) = \phi(y)
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_67.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_68.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              is not constant, there are thus
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_69.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne y \in X
              such that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_70.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x) = \phi(y)
              for all
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_71.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              and so
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_72.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,C,F)
              is not functionally Hausdorff.
            %p
              If a Frölicher space is Hausdorff then smooth functions separate points. Thus for
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_73.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne y \in X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_74.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              with
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_75.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x) = -1
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_76.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(y) = 1
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_77.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^{-1}(-\infty,0)
              and
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_78.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^{-1}(0,\infty)
              are sufficient to show that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_79.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with the functional topology is Hausdorff. As the curvaceous topology is stronger than the functional one, it is thus also Hausdorff.
            %p
              Suppose that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_80.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with the curvaceous topology is Hausdorff. Then any finite subset is discrete and so there are no non-constant continuous maps
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_81.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to X
              with finite image. In particular, there are no non-constant smooth maps and so the original Frölicher space was Hausdorff.
          %p
            In light of this, we shall refer to just
            %em Hausdorff
            Frölicher spaces.
          %p Just as with topological spaces, there is a “Hausdorffification” functor. Unlike topological spaces, this functor is split.
          #LemHausFunct.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_5 Lemma
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_82.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,C,F)
              be a Frölicher space. Let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_83.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              be the quotient of
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_84.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              by the relation
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_85.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \sim y
              if
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_86.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x) = \phi(y)
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_87.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_88.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              inherits a Frölicher space structure from
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_89.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with respect to which it is Hausdorff. The natural map
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_90.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
              is a quotient mapping in the category of Frölicher spaces. It is split, but not canonically so. However, any two splittings are related by a diffeomorphism on
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_91.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              The assignment
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_92.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto Y
              is left adjoint to the inclusion of the category of Hausdorff Frölicher spaces in the category of all Frölicher spaces.
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              The Frölicher structure on
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_93.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is defined by setting
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_94.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_Y
              to be the set of functions
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_95.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : Y \to \mathbb{R}
              such that the composition
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_96.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi ϕ
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y \xrightarrow{\phi} \mathbb{R}
              is in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_97.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_98.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              projects down to a smooth curve in
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_99.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              which explains why this family of functions on
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_100.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is also saturated and hence we have a Frölicher space structure on
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_101.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %p
              To show that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_102.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is Hausdorff, we merely observe that by slight abuse of notation,
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_103.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_X = F_Y
              so if
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_104.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y \in X
              are such that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_105.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi y
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(\overline{x}) = \phi(\overline{y})
              for all
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_106.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F_Y
              then
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_107.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x) = \phi(y)
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_108.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F_X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_109.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mi y
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{x} = \overline{y}
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_110.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %p
              That this is a quotient is straightforward. Any smooth map
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_111.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : X \to Z
              which factors through
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_112.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %em as a set
              must also do so as a Frölicher space. In particular, if
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_113.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : X \to Z
              is a smooth map with
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_114.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              Hausdorff then this must factor through
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_115.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              as a set, whence as a Frölicher space. This also establishes the necessary adjunction.
            %p
              Finally, let us look at the splitting. For each point in
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_116.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              choose a representative of the equivalence class. This choice defines a map on the underlying sets
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_117.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_118.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_X
              and
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_119.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_Y
              are identified by the quotient mapping
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_120.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
            %p
              Given two such splittings, say
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_121.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                      %mo :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i, j : Y \to X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_122.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to X
              which interchanges
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_123.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(y)
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_124.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j(y)
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_125.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              and
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_126.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              are splits of the quotient mapping
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_127.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
              this is well-defined. It is also clearly a diffeomorphism since
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_128.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_X
              cannot detect the difference between
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_129.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_130.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
          %p
            This shows, incidentally, that every smooth curve in the Hausdorffification of
            %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_131.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            lifts to a smooth curve in
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_132.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em usually
            happen with quotients in the category of Frölicher spaces.
          %p The fibres of the Hausdorffification are straightforward to identify.
          #FibHaus.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_6 Lemma
            %p The fibres of the Hausdorffification of a Frölicher space correspond precisely to the maximal subsets which inherit an indiscrete structure from the ambient space.
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %h6#proof_6 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_133.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a Frölicher space,
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_134.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              its Hausdorffification. For
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_135.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in Y
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_136.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_y
              be the corresponding fibre. Then
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_137.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_y
              inherits a Frölicher space structure from its inclusion in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_138.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_139.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_y
              are those that are smooth when considered as curves in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_140.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_141.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : \mathbb{R} \to X_y
              be an arbitrary curve. Then for
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_142.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F_X
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_143.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} im
                      %mi α
                      %mo ⊆
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \im \alpha \subseteq X_y
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_144.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \circ \alpha
              is constant. Hence
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_145.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              is smooth as a curve in
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_146.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_147.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_y
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_148.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_y
              is indiscrete.
            %p
              Conversely, let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_149.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z \subseteq X
              be a subset that inherits an indiscrete structure from
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_150.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_151.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y \in Z
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_152.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              in
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_153.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_154.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} im
                      %mi α
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \im \alpha = \{x,y\}
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_155.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              so for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_156.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F_X
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_157.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x) = \phi(y)
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_158.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              is contained in a (unique) fibre of the quotient map
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_159.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
          %p
            Thus when we pass to the Hausdorffification we lose almost no information at all and one could certainly say that we lose no
            %em interesting
            information.
          %p Having dealt with Hausdorff Frölicher spaces, the obvious next thing to do is to consider the other separation properties. Our next definition may be a little surprising at first.
          #FroReg.num_defn{:style => "border: solid #cccccc; border-width: 2px 1px; padding: 0 1em; margin: 0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A Frölicher space is said to be
              %em regular
              if the curvaceous and functional topologies agree.
          %p
            The point of this definition is that for the underlying topological space of a Frölicher space what one really wants to know is not whether or not it is regular but whether or not it is
            %em smoothly
            regular. This is automatic for the functional topology so the only reasonable question is whether or not it happens for the curvaceous topology. However, a topology is smoothly regular if and only if the smooth functions generate the topology which means that the curvaceous topology is smoothly regular if and only if it agrees with the functional topology. Hence the definition.
          %h1#compactness Compactness
          %p
            It is straightforward to see what one version of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compactness
            should be.
          #FunComp.num_defn{:style => "border: solid #cccccc; border-width: 2px 1px; padding: 0 1em; margin: 0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              A Frölicher space is
              %em functionally compact
              if every smooth function has bounded image.
          %p The images are automatically compact as a smooth function with non-compact image can be converted to a smooth function with unbounded image by suitable composition.
          #LemFunComp.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_7 Lemma
            %p A Frölicher space is functionally compact if and only if the functional topology is compact.
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %h6#proof_7 Proof
            %p One way is obvious: if the functional topology is compact then as the smooth functions are continuous, they have compact image, hence bounded.
            %p
              For the converse, assume that the functional topology is not compact. Then we can find a countable family of points
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_160.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_n)
              with no accumulation points. As the functional topology is (smoothly) regular, we can find smooth functions
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_161.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi_n)
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_162.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_n(x_m) = \delta_{n m}
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_163.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi n
                      %mover
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mi n
                        %mo ^
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum n \widehat{\phi_n}
          #CurvComp.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_8 Lemma
            %p The curvaceous topology of a Frölicher space is compact if and only if the Frölicher space is functionally compact and regular.
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %h6#proof_8 Proof
            %p Since the topologies on a Frölicher space are the pull-backs of the topologies on the Hausdorffification, it is sufficient to prove this for a Hausdorff Frölicher space.
            %p As the curvaceous topology is stronger than the functional, if the curvaceous topology is compact then so is the functional. Moreover, as both are Hausdorff spaces, the identity map is a continuous bijection from a compact space to a Hausdorff space and hence a homeomorphism. Thus the Frölicher space is regular.
            %p Conversely, if the Frölicher space is regular its topologies agree and thus if it is functionally compact then its curvaceous topology is compact.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds+of+mapping+spaces"} manifolds of mapping spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_164.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \times X
          #ProdSeqCpt.num_proposition
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_165.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a Frölicher space. The curvaceous topology on
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_166.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is sequentially compact if and only if
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_167.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \times X
              has the following property.
            %p
              A subset
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_168.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq \mathbb{R} \times X
              containing
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_169.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\} \times X
              is a neighbourhood of
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_170.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\} \times X
              if and only if it contains a subset of the form
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_171.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-\epsilon,\epsilon) \times X
              for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_172.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \gt 0
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %h6#proof_9 Proof
            %p
              Suppose that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_173.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is sequentially compact. Let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_174.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq \mathbb{R} \times X
              be a subset containing
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_175.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\} \times X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_176.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              does not contain a subset of the form
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_177.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-\epsilon,\epsilon) \times X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_178.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              we can find some
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_179.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t_n, x_n) \in U
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_180.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} abs
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi t
                          %mi n
                      %mo ≤
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \abs{t_n} \le \frac1n
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_181.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_n)
              in
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_182.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              has a convergent subsequence, say
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_183.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_{n_k}) \to x
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_184.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi k
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t_{n_k}, x_{n_k})
              converges in
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_185.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \times X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_186.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi k
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∉
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t_{n_k}, x_{n_k}) \notin U
              for all
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_187.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              but its limit,
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_188.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,x)
              is in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_189.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_190.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is not open.
            %p
              Conversely, assume that neighbourhoods of
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_191.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\} \times X
              contain slices as claimed. Then let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_192.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_n)
              be a sequence in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_193.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_194.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              formed by taking
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_195.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \times X
              and removing
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_196.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\frac1n, x_n)
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_197.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-\epsilon,\epsilon) \times X
              and so cannot be open in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_198.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \times X
            %p
              As the set
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_199.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is not open, there must be a curve
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_200.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \colon \mathbb{R} \to U
              which detects the fact that it is not open. That is,
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_201.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^{-1}(U)
              is not open in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_202.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_203.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (s_k) \to s
              in
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_204.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              such that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_205.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∉
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (s_k) \notin c^{-1}(U)
              but
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_206.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in U
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_207.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(s_k) \notin U
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_208.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi k
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(s_k) = (t_{n_k}, x_{n_k})
              for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_209.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_k
              = succeed "s" do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_210.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_k
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_211.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (s_k) \to s
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_212.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(s_k) \to c(s)
              and so
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_213.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi k
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi X
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_{n_k} \to p_X c(s)
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_214.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
