              is sequentially compact.
          %h1#connectedness Connectedness
          %p Another obvious topological property is connectedness. Here it is obvious what the two definitions should be.
          #FroConnect.num_defn{:style => "border: solid #cccccc; border-width: 2px 1px; padding: 0 1em; margin: 0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              A Frölicher space is
              %em functionally connected
              if the only idempotents in its algebra of functions are the trivial ones.
            %p
              More generally, the
              %em functional connected components
              of a Frölicher space
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_215.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,C,F)
              are the equivalence classes of the relation
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_216.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \sim y
              if whenever
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_217.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              is idempotent then
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_218.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x) = \phi(y)
            %p
              A Frölicher space is
              %em curvaceously connected
              if every pair of points lie on a curve.
            %p
              More generally, the
              %em curvaceous connected components
              of a Frölicher space
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_219.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,C,F)
              are the equivalence classes of the relation
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_220.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \sim y
              if there is a smooth curve
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_221.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in C
              with
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_222.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(0) = x
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_223.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(1) = y
          %p That the second relation is an equivalence relation follows from the fact that piecewise smooth curves can be reparametrised to smooth curves.
          #LemConnect.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_9 Lemma
            %p The notions of functionally connected and curvaceously connected coincide.
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %h6#proof_10 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_224.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,C,F)
              be a Frölicher space. It is clear that if
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_225.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in X
              are such that there is a smooth curve connecting them then
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_226.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x) = \phi(y)
              for any idempotent
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_227.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_228.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X' \subseteq X
              be a curvaceously connected component of
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_229.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_230.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon X \to \mathbb{R}
              be the characteristic function of
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_231.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X'
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_232.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in C
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_233.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} im
                    %mi α
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \im \alpha \subseteq X'
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_234.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} im
                      %mi α
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi X
                      %mo ′
                      %mo =
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \im \alpha \cap X' = \emptyset
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_235.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \circ \alpha
              is a constant function. Hence
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_236.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in F
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_237.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_238.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              are in different curvaceously connected components there is an idempotent element of
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_239.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              separating them. Hence the two notions are the same.
          #CorConnect.num_corollary
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p The functional and curvaceous topologies have the same connected components, and these are the same as the path-connected components.
          %h1#hausdorff More on Hausdorff Frölicher Spaces
          %p
            There is not a great deal of difference between a Hausdorff Frölicher space and a generic one. Much less than the case with topological spaces. To pass from all Frölicher spaces to Hausdorff Frölicher spaces involves only collapsing everything that is
            = succeed "." do
              %em indiscrete
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_240.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set
          %p
            On the other hand, the relationship between the category of Hausdorff Frölicher spaces and that of all Frölicher spaces is very good. Not only is it a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            with all that that implies, but the morphisms from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit natural transformation
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} split epimorphisms
            (though not naturally split).
          %p The category of Hausdorff Frölicher spaces is thus complete and co-complete. It is also cartesian closed since the product and exponential objects of Hausdorff Frölicher spaces are again Hausdorff.
          .query
            %p Do I need to prove this, or is it automatic? (I can prove it if necessary)
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike
              \: Completeness and cocompleteness are of course automatic. It is not automatic that a reflective subcategory inherits cartesian closure; the closest thing I can think of is A4.3.1 in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
              which says that a reflective subcategory is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+ideal"} exponential ideal
              iff its reflector preserves finite products.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Stacey"}> Andrew
              \: Is it true, then, that all I need to do is to prove that the exponential of one Hausdorff object by another Hausdorff object is again Hausdorff?
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike
              \: Yes, that would certainly suffice to show that the category of Hausdorff objects is cartesian closed.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Stacey"}> Andrew
              \: Ah, and now I see from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+ideal"} exponential ideal
              that I could just show that the Hausdorffification of a product is the product of the Hausdorffifications. Both seem quite simple, not sure which is the simplest.
          %p
            In considering
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} Isbell duality
            in the context of Frölicher spaces we saw one good reason to restrict to Hausdorff Frölicher spaces. Another reason comes from the inclusion of the category of manifolds in that of Frölicher spaces. This factors through Hausdorff Frölicher spaces and this inclusion has some very pleasant properties.
          #Manifolds.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p The inclusion functor from the category of Manifolds to that of Hausdorff Frölicher spaces preserves limits and colimits.
          .proof{:style => "margin-left: 2em;"}
            %p
              Let us write
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_241.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              for the category of manifolds,
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_242.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              for the category of Hausdorff Frölicher spaces, and
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_243.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
              for the category of all Frölicher spaces. We shall not give the inclusion functors special symbols but trust to context to distinguish. Let
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_244.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F} : I \to \mathcal{M}
              be a functor where
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_245.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is a small category.
            %p
              Let us assume first that
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_246.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
              has a limit in
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_247.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_248.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_0
              with maps
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_249.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝔉
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_i : M_0 \to \mathfrak{F}(i)
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_250.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
              for the limit of
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_251.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
              viewed as a functor into
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_252.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_253.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝔉
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_i : X_0 \to \mathfrak{F}(i)
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_254.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_255.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℱ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_256.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
              is the same as the limit of
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_257.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_258.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℱ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            %p
              Since
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_259.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_0
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_260.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔉
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_261.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : M_0 \to X_0
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_262.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mi i
                      %mi γ
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_i \gamma = \alpha_i
            %p
              As a Frölicher space,
              %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_263.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
              is completely determined by its underlying set and its smooth curves. Its underlying set is (naturally isomorphic to)
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_8119de379c6e6a449dcec44a9d2398d1232435eb_264.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo *
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
